# Generic selection — no static / positional progression

Actenora must stay **generic**: never advance domain logic or tests by magic list positions or “slot N means entity X”.

## Forbidden

- `list.get(0)`, `list.get(1)`, `arr[0]`, `split(...)[0]` when the index encodes **domain meaning**
- Assuming stable order between arrange data and assertions (“first = Murat, second = garbage”)
- Index loops whose purpose is “pick the special one at slot i” instead of a predicate/score
- Hard-wiring pipeline behavior to a fixed ordinal in decisions/actions/commitments/segments

## Required

- Select by **identity or meaning**: id, text/content cue, owner, evidence id, status, hash, or scored preference
- Prefer `stream().filter(...).findFirst()`, `max(Comparator)`, maps keyed by id
- After filtering to a **verified singleton**, `getFirst()` / `orElseThrow()` is fine
- Numbered UI/summary lines may use an iteration counter for display only — not for resolving which entity is which

## Allowed exceptions (narrow)

- External protocol shapes that are defined as “first choice” (e.g. OpenAI `choices[0]`) — wrap once at the adapter boundary
- Mutable accumulators / token counters that are not domain entities

## Examples

```java
// BAD — positional meaning
assertEquals("Murat Sancar", result.actions().get(0).owner());
ActionItemCandidate best = cluster.get(0);

// GOOD — generic
ActionItemCandidate murat = result.actions().stream()
        .filter(a -> a.text().contains("paylaşacak"))
        .findFirst()
        .orElseThrow();
assertEquals("Murat Sancar", murat.owner());

ActionItemCandidate best = cluster.stream()
        .max(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a.text().length()))
        .orElseThrow();
```
